            <ArticleTitle>Epidemiology and mechanisms of gastroesophageal reflux disease in the elderly: a perspective.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>230-234</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/nyas.13196</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) seems to be more common in older individuals. Disturbed esophageal motility; anatomical disorders, such as hiatal hernia, increased obesity, defective mucosal integrity, and alteration in salivary secretion; and concomitant medications all attribute to the higher rate of GERD in the elderly. In this review, we discuss the underlying mechanisms that could explain this higher rate of GERD in the elderly.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2016 New York Academy of Sciences.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
